  Read the LEVELS with care — they are the part version 1 got wrong.
  Learning does NOT mean 'shorter': on an active factor the right
  lengthscale is near the feature width, which is close to where the MAP
  attractor already sits, so the level barely has to move. On an inert
  factor the right lengthscale is LONG. That is why 2.1 decides and not this.

====================================================================================================
2.3  DID IT COST ANYTHING?  (n=46, the model that chose the final batch)
====================================================================================================
  SHAPE SKILL = 1 - var(residual)/var(truth) along a slice through the true
  optimum. A shape-blind predictor scores 0 whatever its level error, so
  active and inert are comparable and so are d=6 and d=8. Raw slice RMSE is
  NOT reported: it is ~97% level error and its active/inert ordering is
  fixed by the oracle's weights, identical in the winning and losing cells.
  CAVEAT: skill on INERT coordinates has a near-zero denominator by
  construction — the truth is almost flat there — so it goes large and
  negative and is NOT comparable to the active column. Only the ACTIVE
  column is interpretable as a skill; the inert one is reported for
  completeness and should be read as 'the GP moves where the truth does not'.

         cell              skill act            skill inert  level err  x_opt->train
   d=6 s=0.25    0.162 [0.010,0.385]  -0.144 [-5.972,0.197]     0.2798        0.4047
    d=6 s=0.1    0.530 [0.195,0.669]   0.007 [-1.580,0.344]     0.1543        0.3267
   d=8 s=0.25    0.215 [0.048,0.420] -1.206 [-11.534,0.291]     0.2578        0.4309
    d=8 s=0.1    0.471 [0.252,0.652]  -0.499 [-4.519,0.275]     0.1817        0.5038

         cell   |argmax-opt| act     null  CF gamma |  proposals: act 1st      2nd     null |  @bound act  @bound inert
   d=6 s=0.25             0.1976   0.3327    0.1766 |              0.2662   0.2422   0.3327 |       0.062         0.145
    d=6 s=0.1             0.1257   0.3327    0.1215 |              0.2318   0.1934   0.3327 |       0.059         0.251
   d=8 s=0.25             0.1775   0.3377    0.1655 |              0.2409   0.2333   0.3377 |       0.051         0.120
    d=8 s=0.1             0.1438   0.3377    0.1440 |              0.2087   0.1862   0.3377 |       0.047         0.289

  All distances are per-coordinate RMS on the ACTIVE subspace, so d=6 and
  d=8 are comparable and the nuisance coordinates BO is right to ignore do
  not dilute the signal. 'null' = a uniform random point in the box.
  '@bound' is the fraction of proposed COORDINATE VALUES on a face, split by
  group: an inert coordinate on a bound is near-costless by the oracle's own
  design and must not be pooled with an active one. No uniform reference is
  quoted — qLogEI is a bounded maximiser of an acquisition whose exploration
  term peaks at faces, so a uniform draw is not its operative null.

====================================================================================================
2.4  COUNTERFACTUAL: the same recovered designs, Gamma(3,6) lengthscale prior
====================================================================================================
  No campaign is re-run and no E2 number is touched. Without a condition
  that varies the prior, no lengthscale value can attribute anything TO the
  prior. Gamma(3,6) penalises ls=10 by ~51 nats/dim where the shipped prior
  penalises it by ~1.5, so this isolates the tail penalty; their modes are
  0.502 and 0.333, nearly the same place.

